PG.notes("l242", "<p class=\"summary\">First hands-on SQL lesson in an online SQLite playground. Recreates a products table with CREATE TABLE (including data types, PRIMARY KEY and NOT NULL), then adds rows with INSERT INTO using both the full-values and the named-columns forms, and shows the NOT NULL error.</p>\n<h3>Setup and vocabulary</h3>\n<ul><li>Docs: the W3Schools SQL reference (w3schools.com/sql) is the suggested place to look up syntax. Treat SQL as a new language with its own keywords.</li><li>SQL keywords (SELECT, FROM, CREATE, TABLE, INSERT INTO ...) are conventionally written in uppercase. The playground colours recognised keywords, which is a quick typo check.</li><li>CRUD: Create, Read, Update, Destroy (delete). Every database is first learned by practising these four operations.</li><li>Environment: sqliteonline.com, an in-browser SQLite playground linked in the lecture resources. It starts with a sample customers table (id, first name, last name, address, two rows). Right-click a table and choose Show table to view its rows, or SQL Schema to see its definition. Delete the sample code in the editor before writing your own.</li><li>Terminology: a table has columns (fields) that define its structure (the schema), and each row is a record.</li></ul>\n<h3>CREATE TABLE</h3>\n<ul><li>Form: CREATE TABLE table_name ( column1 datatype, column2 datatype, ... ). Keywords first, then the table name, then round brackets listing each column with its data type.</li><li>A column definition is name, a space, then the type. No colons (that is JavaScript object syntax), and columns are separated by commas.</li><li>id INT: integer type, used as the unique identifier for each product.</li><li>name: a text column. The lecture writes it as a string type; the docs list text types such as CHAR(255), which limits a value to 255 characters. (Extra) In real schemas use TEXT or VARCHAR(n); SQLite is lenient about type names, but PostgreSQL later in the course is not.</li><li>price MONEY: a currency-style number type listed in the docs' data types section (alongside SMALLMONEY). (Extra) MONEY is not available in SQLite as a real type and is not recommended for exact decimals elsewhere; NUMERIC or DECIMAL are the usual choices.</li><li>PRIMARY KEY (id): a final line inside the brackets that marks id as the column uniquely identifying each record. Product 1 is then always one specific row and no other row can reuse that id.</li><li>NOT NULL after a column's type forbids a record without a value in that column. It is paired with the primary key because a record with no id could never be identified later.</li><li>Check before running: commas between columns, no colons, brackets balanced. If Run reports an error, read the message and compare against the code for typos.</li></ul>\n<h3>INSERT INTO</h3>\n<ul><li>Two forms. Full: INSERT INTO table VALUES (v1, v2, v3) supplies a value for every column in schema order. Named: INSERT INTO table (colA, colB) VALUES (a, b) supplies only the listed columns.</li><li>Text values go in single quotes; numbers do not. Values are comma separated inside round brackets.</li><li>Columns left out of a named insert become NULL (no value). Example: a pencil with id and name but no price shows a NULL price when the table is shown.</li><li>The NOT NULL constraint is enforced: inserting only name and price, leaving id out, fails with 'NOT NULL constraint failed: products.id'. Constraints act as built-in validation so the data keeps to the structure declared at creation.</li><li>The lecture writes each statement without a trailing semicolon and notes that a statement is really one logical line even when split across lines for readability. (Extra) A closing semicolon is standard practice and required by many clients, including psql.</li></ul>\n<h3>Result after the lesson</h3>\n<ul><li>products table with columns id, name, price.</li><li>Row 1: id 1, Pen, price 1.20. Row 2: id 2, Pencil, price NULL.</li><li>The next lesson covers reading and searching the table.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Create the products table (SQLite playground)</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE products (\n  id INT NOT NULL,\n  name TEXT,\n  price MONEY,\n  PRIMARY KEY (id)\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Insert rows: all columns, then only some columns</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">INSERT INTO products VALUES (1, 'Pen', 1.20);\n\n-- price unknown yet, so it becomes NULL\nINSERT INTO products (id, name) VALUES (2, 'Pencil');\n\n-- fails: id is NOT NULL and not supplied\nINSERT INTO products (name, price) VALUES ('Rubber', 1.30);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Create and populate a products table</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Open sqliteonline.com. It has a sample customers table. Clear the editor.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Recreate the products table from the previous lesson in real SQL and add two products.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Write CREATE TABLE products with id (integer, NOT NULL), name (text) and price (money), and finish with PRIMARY KEY (id).</li><li>Run it, then right-click products and choose Show table; it should be empty.</li><li>Insert the first product with the full-values form: id 1, 'Pen', 1.20.</li><li>Insert a second product, id 2 and 'Pencil', naming only id and name so price is NULL.</li><li>Show the table to confirm both rows, then deliberately insert a row without id and read the NOT NULL error.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A two-row products table, with a NULL price on the Pencil row, and an error when id is missing.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE products (id INT NOT NULL, name TEXT, price MONEY, PRIMARY KEY (id));\nINSERT INTO products VALUES (1, 'Pen', 1.20);\nINSERT INTO products (id, name) VALUES (2, 'Pencil');</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using colons or JavaScript-style syntax in the column list.</li><li>Missing commas between column definitions.</li><li>Forgetting quotes around text values, or quoting numbers.</li><li>Omitting the NOT NULL id and then getting a constraint failure on insert.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Using a full-values INSERT with values in the wrong order or count.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l243", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the R of CRUD in SQL: SELECT to read all or chosen columns, and WHERE to filter rows by a condition.</p>\n<h3>SELECT</h3>\n<ul><li>SELECT reads data. The playground's Show table action simply runs SELECT * FROM products behind the scenes.</li><li>The asterisk is a wildcard meaning every column. FROM names the table to read.</li><li>To read only some columns, list them separated by commas instead of the asterisk: SELECT name, price FROM products. The result omits the other columns (here id). The table itself is unchanged.</li></ul>\n<h3>WHERE</h3>\n<ul><li>WHERE adds a search condition and limits the result to rows that satisfy it: SELECT * FROM products WHERE id = 1 returns just the row whose id is 1.</li><li>Text comparisons use single-quoted strings, e.g. WHERE Country = 'Mexico'. The same clause works with column lists in place of the asterisk.</li><li>SQL has more operators for conditions: equals, greater than, less than, BETWEEN, LIKE and others, listed in the W3Schools documentation. (Extra) Conditions can be combined with AND / OR.</li><li>SELECT with WHERE will be used constantly when working with SQL databases.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reading data</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">SELECT * FROM products;                    -- every column, every row\nSELECT name, price FROM products;          -- only two columns\nSELECT * FROM products WHERE id = 1;       -- only matching rows\nSELECT name FROM products WHERE price &gt; 1; -- (Extra) comparison operator</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Using a double = sign as in JavaScript; SQL uses a single = for comparison.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Double-quoting string values in WHERE; single quotes are the standard for string literals.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l244", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the U of CRUD: UPDATE ... SET ... WHERE to change existing records, and ALTER TABLE ... ADD to add a column. Ends with a small challenge that fills a new stock column for two products.</p>\n<h3>UPDATE</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: UPDATE table SET column = value WHERE condition. UPDATE names the table, SET lists the new values, and WHERE restricts which rows are touched.</li><li>Use case: the pencil row was inserted with a NULL price; UPDATE products SET price = 0.8 WHERE id = 2 gives it one.</li><li>Danger: without WHERE the statement applies to every row, so UPDATE products SET price = 0.8 would overwrite all prices. Always include WHERE, ideally on the primary key, and check it before running.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Several columns can be set in one statement by separating assignments with commas.</li></ul>\n<h3>ALTER TABLE ... ADD</h3>\n<ul><li>ALTER TABLE changes the table's structure (add, delete or modify columns) rather than a record.</li><li>To add a column give the table name, the ADD keyword, the new column name and its data type: ALTER TABLE products ADD stock INTEGER.</li><li>Every existing row receives NULL in the new column, so existing records need a follow-up UPDATE.</li><li>The lecture's takeaway: patching in missing columns and back-filling values is tedious, so plan the schema carefully at creation time.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Update one row, add a column, back-fill it</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">UPDATE products SET price = 0.8 WHERE id = 2;\n\nALTER TABLE products ADD stock INTEGER;\n\nUPDATE products SET stock = 32 WHERE id = 1;\nUPDATE products SET stock = 12 WHERE id = 2;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Fill the stock column</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> products table with Pen (id 1) and Pencil (id 2). After ALTER TABLE, a stock column exists with NULL in both rows.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Give each product its stock count: 32 pens and 12 pencils.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Write UPDATE products SET stock = 32 WHERE id = 1 and run it.</li><li>Clear the editor, write UPDATE products SET stock = 12 WHERE id = 2 and run it.</li><li>Run SELECT * FROM products to confirm no NULLs remain.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Pen has stock 32 and Pencil stock 12; pencil price 0.8; no NULL values anywhere.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">UPDATE products SET stock = 32 WHERE id = 1;\nUPDATE products SET stock = 12 WHERE id = 2;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Running UPDATE without WHERE changes every row.</li><li>Forgetting that a new column is NULL for existing rows until updated.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l245", "<p class=\"summary\">Completes CRUD with DELETE FROM ... WHERE, and warns about omitting the WHERE clause.</p>\n<h3>DELETE FROM</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: DELETE FROM table_name WHERE condition removes every row that matches the condition.</li><li>Example: remove the pencil with DELETE FROM products WHERE name = 'Pencil'. Matching on the unique id is safer and clearer: DELETE FROM products WHERE id = 2. Names can repeat; a primary key cannot.</li><li>Without WHERE the statement deletes all rows in the table, so the data is lost. Re-read the statement before running it.</li><li>After the delete only the pen row remains. With INSERT, SELECT, UPDATE and DELETE the full CRUD set has now been covered in SQL.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> DELETE removes rows only; the table and its columns stay. Removing the table itself is a separate command (DROP TABLE).</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Delete by id (preferred) and the dangerous form</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">DELETE FROM products WHERE id = 2;\n\n-- DANGER: no WHERE, empties the whole table\n-- DELETE FROM products;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Running DELETE with no WHERE clause wipes the table's rows.</li><li>Filtering on a non-unique column (such as name) can remove more than one record.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l246", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds a relational design in the SQLite playground: an orders table whose customer_id and product_id are foreign keys to customers and products, then uses INNER JOIN to merge related rows into one result. Includes a challenge to join orders with products.</p>\n<h3>Restore the pencil row (mini challenge)</h3>\n<ul><li>If the pencil was deleted in the previous lesson, insert it again with the full-values form: INSERT INTO products VALUES (2, 'Pencil', 0.8, 12). The four values follow the column order id, name, price, stock.</li></ul>\n<h3>The orders table and foreign keys</h3>\n<ul><li>Columns: id (INT, NOT NULL, the primary key), order_number (INT), customer_id (INT) and product_id (INT).</li><li>A foreign key is a column holding the primary key value of a row in another table. It links the two tables: customer_id says which customer placed the order, product_id says which product was ordered. This is how a relationship is expressed in SQL, and it avoids copying customer or product details into every order.</li><li>Declare them with FOREIGN KEY (column) REFERENCES other_table (its_primary_key_column), one declaration per foreign key, after the column list and the PRIMARY KEY (id) line. For this lesson: FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers (id) and FOREIGN KEY (product_id) REFERENCES products (id).</li><li>Syntax errors show in the editor's output; the lecture's own typo was a curly brace used instead of a round bracket. Fix and re-run.</li><li>Show Schema (right-click the table) lists four fields: the primary key plus the two foreign keys.</li></ul>\n<h3>Inserting a related record</h3>\n<ul><li>First order: INSERT INTO orders VALUES (1, 4362, 2, 1), meaning order id 1, order number 4362, customer id 2 (Angela Yu, 12 Sunset Drive in the sample customers table) and product id 1.</li><li>The numbers are just references; the details live in the customers and products tables and are pulled together by a join.</li></ul>\n<h3>INNER JOIN</h3>\n<ul><li>JOIN combines rows from two tables. Types include LEFT, RIGHT, FULL and INNER; INNER JOIN is the most used and keeps only rows where the matching condition holds in both tables.</li><li>Structure: SELECT the wanted columns, written as table.column to say which table each comes from; FROM the table holding the foreign key; INNER JOIN the second table; ON the matching condition: foreign key = primary key of the other table.</li><li>Example result columns: orders.order_number plus customers.first_name, customers.last_name, customers.address, matched by orders.customer_id = customers.id. One result row then tells you who an order is for and where to send it.</li><li>The joined result is a temporary combined table produced by the query; the stored tables are not changed.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge: join orders with products</h3>\n<ul><li>Swap the second table for products and select orders.order_number, products.name, products.price and products.stock.</li><li>The ON condition becomes orders.product_id = products.id. The result shows the order number alongside the product details.</li><li>A prebuilt copy of the full database (customers, products, orders) is linked in the lesson resources for experimenting.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">orders table with two foreign keys</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE orders (\n  id INT NOT NULL,\n  order_number INT,\n  customer_id INT,\n  product_id INT,\n  PRIMARY KEY (id),\n  FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers (id),\n  FOREIGN KEY (product_id) REFERENCES products (id)\n);\n\nINSERT INTO orders VALUES (1, 4362, 2, 1);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Inner join with customers</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">SELECT orders.order_number, customers.first_name, customers.last_name, customers.address\nFROM orders\nINNER JOIN customers ON orders.customer_id = customers.id;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Join orders with products</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> customers, products (with stock column) and orders tables, with one order (4362, customer 2, product 1).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Produce a result containing the order number with the product's name, price and stock.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Start from the customers join query.</li><li>Replace the selected columns with orders.order_number, products.name, products.price, products.stock.</li><li>Replace the joined table with products.</li><li>Change the ON condition to orders.product_id = products.id and run.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> One row: order number 4362 with the name, price and stock of product 1.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">SELECT orders.order_number, products.name, products.price, products.stock\nFROM orders\nINNER JOIN products ON orders.product_id = products.id;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Syntax slips such as the wrong bracket type stop CREATE TABLE from running; read the error message.</li><li>Mismatched ON condition: it must pair the foreign key with the other table's primary key.</li><li>Column names without a table prefix can be ambiguous when both tables share names (such as id).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The lecture says the order is for a pencil, but product id 1 is the Pen; the data and the spoken description disagree, so trust the ids.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> SQLite does not enforce foreign keys unless PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON; PostgreSQL does.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l247", "<p class=\"summary\">Study-habit tip: work where other people are also working to boost motivation.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Studying alone at home makes it easy to put off hard tasks. Being around focused people raises your own effort.</li><li>Options: a study buddy, a university or public library, or any quiet place where others are working. Alumni cards can keep university libraries accessible.</li><li>Use the setting for the tasks you tend to procrastinate on, and keep going consistently.</li><li>No technical content.</li></ul>");
